  "response": "Disputes of this kind are adjudicated first by arithmetic and only afterwards by interpretation, because every account of causes presupposes a curve to be explained. Stark estimated that the Christian population grew at roughly 40 percent per decade, and Stark calculated an annual growth rate of 40 percent for the Christian population; these two measures cannot both be true, since compounding at two-fifths every year rules out an increment of that same size spread across ten. Because the decadal rate is what his projection compounds, Stark's model yields approximately 6.3 million Christians by the year 300 CE; that is to say, Stark's figures place Christians at roughly ten percent of the empire's population around 300 CE. Hopkins, by contrast, estimated about 20 million Christians in the Roman Empire by 300 CE.\n\nOnce a curve has been fixed, the argument moves to mechanism, and here the doctrinal and the sociological explanations are usually pressed in their strongest forms. Stark argued that conversion normally travelled along pre-existing interpersonal and kinship ties, and MacMullen emphasized reports of miracles and exorcisms as drivers of conversion; these two accounts cannot both be true, since recruitment that ran as a rule through family and friendship networks rules out recruitment by the broadcast appeal of wonders. Christian congregations distributed food and burial funds to destitute members, a practice confirmed by the mid-third-century episcopal testimony adduced in a moment.\n\nThe decisive question for the social account is whether the conditions of the surrounding society can be shown to have given the movement's teaching its purchase, and mortality crises are the favoured test case. The Antonine Plague began afflicting the Roman Empire in 165 CE, and Cyprian of Carthage described Christian care for plague victims during the 250s; an epidemic that struck the provinces in the 160s cannot be squared with eyewitness description of its victims being nursed nearly a century later, so one of the two datings must give way. Differential survival among the nursed and the unnursed would, over a few generations, alter the religious composition of whole cities, which is why the chronology of epidemics and the chronology of organised charity are both scrutinised so closely.\n\nTextual attestation of communities, however, remains the backbone of the discipline. Pliny the Younger reported on Christians in Bithynia-Pontus around 112 CE, and that governor's testimony confirms the city-by-city documentation of early Christian communities which Adolf von Harnack assembled. The Egyptian documentary material, by contrast with the provincial correspondence just adduced, yields proportions rather than mere attestations of presence, and so speaks to magnitude as well as distribution. Roger Bagnall used personal names in Egyptian papyri to estimate the Christian share of the population. The house church at Dura-Europos was converted for Christian use in the 240s CE. The adaptation of a private dwelling for congregational use in a Mesopotamian garrison town confirms the city-by-city reconstruction described above, since a settlement on the empire's eastern edge turns out to hold precisely the kind of community that the catalogue had placed there.\n\nWhat settles the controversy, then, is convergence rather than elegance. Where counted names, dated buildings and local literary notices agree on the size and spread of the movement at a given date, the range of admissible explanations narrows sharply; where they diverge, as the estimates for 300 CE conspicuously do, no appeal to the attractiveness of doctrine or to the misery of the cities can be more than a conjecture awaiting better numbers.",
